CALIBRATION HARNESS, no graded answer. The verdict is visual and always was: the
script captures three screenshots at ticks 0 / 1000 / 2000 and a human reads the HUD
clock in each PNG. Nothing in the Lua evaluates a bar, so there is no result to grade.

WHY SKIP AND NOT PASS. Until 2026-09-01 the terminal verdict was `Test.Pass`, so this
scenario contributed a green to every `run-batch.sh --all` tally regardless of what the
clock actually read. `Test.Skip` is the harness's own word for "ran, produced no graded
answer" (TestGlobal.cs) and run-batch.sh maps it to the SKIP bucket. The scenario is not
hidden and still runs; the verdict just stops claiming an answer it never computed.

REMOVE THIS FILE in the same commit as whatever gives the scenario a real assertion.
A PASS against a live `skip` declaration grades STALE/RED on purpose -- that asymmetry is
the point: a scenario that STARTS grading shows up loudly instead of silently changing
meaning.

NOT EXPOSED TO expected-status.sh's KNOWN GAP. That gap is specific to `fail`
declarations, where a watchdog TIMEOUT-FAIL also exits 1 and therefore grades green.
A timeout reaches expected_status_grade as FAIL, and declared-`skip` + FAIL is RED, so a
hang here still reds the batch.
